  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Blustery and cold conditions expected tonight \nwith mountain flurries and snow showers. Snow may accumulate 1-3\ninches at elevations above 3500 feet.\n\nA large upper-level low will move from the Ohio Valley into New\nEngland tonight, drawing wrap-around moisture into the region \non its back side. This moisture will bring light snow \naccumulations of 1 to 3 inches across the western slopes and \nhigher elevations. While lower mountain elevations may see a few\npassing flurries, ground temperatures will remain too warm for \nsnow to accumulate on road surfaces.\n\nAs the low continues its northward track overnight, the \nprecipitation will pull away with it. By sunrise on Saturday, \nsnow showers will come to an end and skies will turn mostly \nsunny through the day. However, the departing system will leave \nbehind cold air and gusty winds, dropping Saturday morning \ntemperatures into the 30s. Wind chill values will make it feel \nnoticeably colder, dipping into the upper teens to mid-20s in \nthe mountains and near 30 degrees across the Piedmont.\n\nConditions will improve steadily through Saturday afternoon as \nthe gusty winds die down. Under abundant sunshine, temperatures \nwill rebound nicely, climbing into the upper 40s and lower 50s \nwest of the Blue Ridge, while areas to the east will warm into \nthe upper 50s and low 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Temperatures recover to near normal this \nweekend. Next chance of rain is Sunday night. \n\nTemperatures moderate Saturday and Sunday returning to near \nnormal. Flow aloft becomes zonal for Saturday, cold air aloft \nexiting the region with normal diurnal/nocturnal temperature \nprofile becoming re-established. \n\nA cold front will approach the area Sunday and pass overhead\nSunday night. Southwest winds will promote warm air advection\nahead front with increasing moisture resulting in increasing\ncloudiness. Rain then moves into the western mountains during\nthe late afternoon and passing over the remainder of the CWA\nSunday night. Current projections are for a quarter inch or less\nof rain...the front remaining progressive as it crosses the\nforecast area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Dry weather expected Monday through Thursday of \nnext week.\n\nDry weather returns Monday with near seasonal temperatures\nanticipated for most of next week. Zonal flow aloft is forecast\nfor much of the country early in the week. El Nino will enhance\nanother southern stream storm with impacts to California \nTuesday-Wednesday, the energy from this feature progressing east\nand reaching our part of the country by the weekend. \n\nThe CPC Outlook leans into the southern stream remaining active\nwith the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks reflecting above normal \nchances for precipitation from coast to coast but skewed to the \nsouthern CONUS. Not a lot of cold air to be found, so the most \nof the precipitation associated with these systems should be \nrain.",
